from pathlib import Path
import json
p=Path(__file__).with_name('language-curriculum.json');D=json.loads(p.read_text());D['foundations']=[]
items=[
['intro','What Is Graphic Design?','Define a purpose: uses … to …','Graphic design uses graphical elements and design principles to communicate a message.','Graphic design uses visuals to communicate a message.','Graphic design combines visual elements with deliberate organisation to communicate a particular message to a particular audience.','We need students to know when and where the café opens.','The colors, shapes, and words are the ingredients. We arrange them so the opening information is clear.','Then please make the opening time and place easy to find.','Which version tells people when and where to come?',['The abstract arrangement without details.','The invitation with time and location.','Both communicate the same opening facts.'],1,['The abstract arrangement may look interesting, but it does not provide the opening details.','Yes. It communicates the information the client wants viewers to use.','Only the invitation supplies the time and place.'],['Graphic design uses visuals to communicate.','Graphic design uses visuals for communicate.','Graphic design uses visuals to communicating.'],0,'Use “to communicate” for the purpose: to + base verb.','You need students to attend the café opening. They need the date, time, and location.','Ask who the audience is and what they should understand or do. Name one element and one organising choice.','Agree on the message before choosing the decoration.','Who should see this, and what should they do?','University students should know that we open Friday at 4 pm in Talaimari.','I will make the opening time and place clear for university students. I can use type and color, with hierarchy to guide attention. Is attending the opening the main goal?','A blood-donation event needs a poster. Viewers must know where to register and when to arrive. Explain what graphic design will do for this audience.','I would arrange the words, colors, and shapes to make the registration step and arrival time clear. Where should people register?'],
['line','Line','Describe a function: separates … from …','The line separates the date from the location.','The line separates the two details.','A clear dividing line can separate different information while keeping the whole invitation organised.','The date and location are running together.','I added a line between them so readers can see the two pieces of information.','That helps. Keep both details readable.','What does the line do here?',['It changes the actual opening date.','It separates two pieces of information.','It translates the location into another language.'],1,['The line changes the presentation, not the date.','Yes. You can see the division between the two details.','A visual divider does not translate words.'],['The line separates the date from the location.','The line separates the date of the location.','The line separates the date to the location.'],0,'Use “separate X from Y”.','The date and address look like one long sentence. You want a clearer separation.','Show a version with and without a divider. Explain what the line does rather than only naming it.','Use a line to communicate a useful division.','Which pieces of information should be separate?','The date and the street address should be separate.','I can add a dividing line between the date and address. It separates the details without changing the wording. Would you prefer a line or more empty space?','A timetable mixes two days of opening hours. Recommend how a line could help and ask a follow-up question.','A dividing line can separate Monday’s hours from Tuesday’s hours. Should the two days stay in one box or have separate boxes?'],
['color','Color','Describe a visible change: changes / stands out','The accent color makes the opening offer stand out.','The offer has a different color.','Using one contrasting accent can make the offer more noticeable, while leaving the rest of the palette consistent.','The opening offer is getting lost in the poster.','I changed the offer’s color so it stands out from the other information.','Please keep our brand colors around it.','Which detail changed color?',['The opening offer.','The café’s actual location.','Every element in both versions.'],0,['Yes. The new accent singles out the offer.','The location stays the same.','Most of the palette remains unchanged.'],['The offer stand out.','The offer stands out.','The offer standing out.'],1,'With singular “the offer”, use “stands”.','Your opening offer is 50% off. You want it noticed, but the logo must keep its original colors.','Suggest one accent change and explain which element it emphasises.','Use color for an agreed message.','Which part should the accent color emphasise?','The 50% offer. Keep the logo’s colors unchanged.','I can use an accent color for the 50% offer while keeping the logo unchanged. Would you like a phone-size comparison first?','A community notice needs the registration deadline to stand out. Explain one color choice without changing every element.','I would use one contrasting accent for the deadline and keep the other text consistent. Is the deadline the most important detail?'],
['shape','Shape','Describe what you see: is / has','The mark is a flat circle with a clear outline.','The mark is a circle.','A simple two-dimensional shape can form a recognisable mark before we add color, texture, or shading.','Can you show a simple circular mark?','Here is a flat circle. Its boundary gives it a clear shape.','Let’s test it at a small size.','Which statement describes shape rather than suggested depth?',['It has a curved boundary.','It has a rounded three-dimensional surface.','We can physically hold the screen’s circle.'],0,['Yes. Shape describes the two-dimensional boundary here.','Suggested three-dimensional volume belongs to form.','This is a flat image, not a physical object.'],['The mark have a curved outline.','The mark has a curved outline.','The mark having a curved outline.'],1,'Use “has” with the singular subject “the mark”.','You need a simple icon recognisable when small. You want to compare circular and square outlines.','Describe the outlines accurately and offer to test both at a small size.','Describe a shape without inventing its meaning.','Should we compare a circle and a square at the final icon size?','Yes. The icon will appear very small on a phone.','I can compare a circular outline with a square outline at phone size. Which is easier for your audience to recognise?','A transport app needs a simple stop marker. Describe a possible shape and suggest a small-size check.','The marker could use a clear circular shape. I would test the outline at the smallest map size before adding detail.'],
['texture','Texture','Describe appearance: looks + adjective','The patterned surface looks less smooth than the plain surface.','The second surface looks patterned.','The printed pattern suggests texture, even though the screen itself stays physically flat.','Could the packaging look less plain?','A repeated pattern can suggest a textured surface. We should check whether it makes the label harder to read.','Keep the product name clear.','What can we tell from this screen image?',['The physical paper is definitely rough.','The surface looks patterned.','The package smells like tea.'],1,['A picture does not prove how the actual paper feels.','Yes. We can describe the visible appearance.','Smell is not visible.'],['The surface looks textured.','The surface looks texture.','The surface look textured.'],0,'Use an adjective after “looks”: looks textured.','You want a tea label to suggest handmade paper, but the product name must remain clear.','Use appearance language rather than claiming the printed material really feels rough.','Describe texture as a visual impression.','Do you mean a textured look or a physically textured material?','A textured look. We will print on ordinary smooth paper.','I can create a subtle textured look while keeping the name readable. The pattern suggests roughness, but the printed paper will still be smooth.','A café menu uses a wood-grain image behind small text. Describe what you see and explain what needs checking.','The background looks textured, but the pattern may compete with the small text. I would check readability before using it behind the menu details.'],
['space','Space','Quantities: more space / enough space','There is more empty space around the headline in B.','B has more empty space.','The additional empty space separates the headline from nearby elements and gives it room to remain readable.','The heading feels squeezed between the pictures.','I added more empty space around it. The words are easier to pick out.','Good. Keep the heading and both pictures.','What changed while the content stayed the same?',['The amount of empty space.','The actual headline wording.','The number of opening days.'],0,['Yes. The elements have more room around them.','The wording stays unchanged.','This visual is about spacing, not the calendar.'],['There are more space around the heading.','There is more space around the heading.','There is more spaces around the heading.'],1,'In this use, “space” is uncountable: there is more space.','You need to keep the same heading and two pictures, but the current version feels crowded.','Recommend a spacing change without removing the required content.','Explain what empty space contributes.','Which elements must stay in this version?','The heading and both pictures must stay.','I can add more space around the heading while keeping both pictures. Would you like the heading moved slightly higher?','A lesson handout squeezes instructions between two diagrams. Recommend a change using “space” naturally.','There is not enough space around the instructions. I would increase the gap so readers can separate the words from the diagrams.'],
['form','Form','Cause and appearance: makes … look …','The shading makes the circle look like a three-dimensional ball.','The shading makes it look like a ball.','Light and shadow suggest three-dimensional form even though the illustration is drawn on a flat surface.','Can the ball look less flat?','I added shading to suggest a rounded surface.','Please keep the shape and size the same.','Which change suggests depth?',['The shading.','A new opening date.','A translated label.'],0,['Yes. Light and dark areas suggest a rounded surface.','A date does not create visual depth here.','The label is not the source of depth in this example.'],['The shading makes it looks rounded.','The shading makes it look rounded.','The shading makes it to look rounded.'],1,'After “makes it”, use the base verb: makes it look.','You want a ball illustration to suggest depth, but its outline and size should remain unchanged.','Describe how light and shadow can suggest depth without changing the outline.','Distinguish a flat shape from suggested form.','Should I add depth while keeping the outline unchanged?','Yes. Keep the same circular outline and size.','I can keep the outline and add light and shadow to suggest a rounded form. Do you prefer a subtle or stronger shaded effect?','A product illustration looks flat. The client wants depth while keeping the approved silhouette. Explain a suitable change.','I would keep the approved silhouette and add restrained shading to suggest depth. Could you confirm the intended light direction?'],
['typography','Typography','Contrast: stays the same, but … changes','The wording stays the same, but the type style changes.','The words are the same. The type looks different.','Typography changes how the same wording is presented through choices of letterform, size, weight, and spacing.','Please show the same headline in two type styles.','The words are unchanged. The different letterforms give you two typographic options.','Now compare them at phone size.','Which part stays unchanged in this comparison?',['The exact wording.','The letterform style.','The font weight.'],0,['Yes. The comparison isolates typography rather than changing the message.','The type styles visibly differ.','The examples also differ in visual weight.'],['The words stay the same, but the type changes.','The words stays the same, but the type changes.','The words stay the same, but the type change.'],0,'Use plural “words stay” and singular “type changes”.','You need the approved wording kept exactly, but want to compare type styles.','Show the same text in two styles and discuss readability before recommending.','Treat words and their visual presentation as different decisions.','Should I keep the approved wording exactly the same in both versions?','Yes. Only the type style should change.','I’ll keep the wording unchanged and compare two type styles at phone size. Which tone should the headline suggest?','A client asks for a different type style on a poster. The event title and date are already approved. Explain what will and will not change.','I will keep the approved title and date unchanged while comparing two type styles. Could you confirm the tone you want?'],
['contrast','Contrast','Compare differences: different from','The dark circle is different from the lighter circles around it.','The dark circle stands out.','A visible difference in color, scale, shape, or direction can create contrast and draw attention to one element.','One item needs to be easier to notice.','I made that circle darker than its neighbors. The difference creates contrast.','Keep the other circles quieter.','Why is one circle easier to notice in B?',['It differs in color from its neighbors.','Every circle has the same color.','It has disappeared.'],0,['Yes. The color difference creates the contrast.','B contains one visibly darker circle.','The circle remains present and becomes more noticeable.'],['The circle is different from the others.','The circle is different than of the others.','The circle different from the others.'],0,'Use “is different from” for this description.','One option in a group must be noticed first. Keep all the options visible.','Propose a visible difference rather than deleting the competing items.','Use contrast to guide attention.','Which option should be easiest to notice?','The middle option, but the others still need to be visible.','I can give the middle option a stronger contrast while keeping the others visible. Would you prefer a color or size difference?','A price list needs the most popular plan to stand out. Suggest a contrast choice and ask which plan should lead.','We can use a visible color difference for one plan. Which option should be easiest to notice?'],
['hierarchy','Hierarchy','Priority: first / next / then','The invitation stands out first, then the practical details.','People see the headline first.','The difference in visual importance creates a hierarchy that guides viewers from the invitation to the practical details.','What should viewers read first?','The invitation should lead. The opening details can come next.','Yes. The details must still be readable.','What does the hierarchy guide?',['The order in which information gets attention.','The café’s opening hours themselves.','A translation of every word.'],0,['Yes. The design establishes relative importance.','Hierarchy changes the presentation, not the opening hours.','Hierarchy is not a translation process.'],['People notice the invitation first.','People notices the invitation first.','People notice first the invitation yesterday.'],0,'Use plural “people notice”. “First” identifies the intended order of attention.','You want the opening invitation first and the date and location second.','Explain the order of attention and keep the supporting details readable.','Set a hierarchy from the communication goal.','Should the invitation lead or should the opening date lead?','The invitation first; the date and location next.','I’ll give the invitation the strongest emphasis and keep the date and location together below it. Could you review that reading order?','A course advert should lead with the benefit, followed by start date and registration. Explain the intended order.','Viewers should notice the benefit first, then the start date and registration step. I would use size and spacing to make that hierarchy clear.'],
['alignment','Alignment','Shared edge: line up along …','The text blocks line up along the same left edge in B.','The left edges line up.','A shared alignment gives separate blocks a common visual structure and makes the layout easier to follow.','The text blocks look scattered.','I lined them up along one left edge.','Keep the spacing comfortable as well.','What do the blocks share in B?',['A common left edge.','Exactly the same words.','A new language.'],0,['Yes. Their starting positions align.','Alignment does not require identical wording.','The comparison changes positions rather than language.'],['The blocks line up along the same edge.','The blocks lines up along the same edge.','The blocks are line up along the same edge.'],0,'Use plural “blocks line up”.','You want three text blocks to look organised without changing the copy.','Align the blocks on a shared edge and explain where that edge is.','Use alignment to create a clear structure.','Would you like the text blocks to share a left edge or a centre line?','A left edge. Keep the wording as it is.','I can align the blocks along one left edge while keeping the copy unchanged. Would you like the headings aligned there too?','An application form has labels beginning at different positions. Recommend an alignment change and explain it.','I would line the labels up along a common left edge so the form is easier to follow. Should the input fields share a second edge?'],
['balance','Balance','Distribution: on both sides','The visual weight is spread across both sides in B.','B has shapes on both sides.','The distribution of visual weight can make a composition feel balanced without requiring every object to be identical.','The composition feels heavy on one side.','I spread the visual weight across both sides.','Could an unequal arrangement work too?','Which version distributes weight across the frame?',['A, with all the large shapes on the left.','B, with large shapes on both sides.','Neither version contains any shapes.'],1,['A concentrates the weight on one side.','Yes. B distributes it more evenly across the frame.','Both versions contain visible shapes.'],['The shapes are on both sides.','The shapes is on both sides.','The shapes are in both side.'],0,'Use plural “shapes are” and “both sides”.','You like an informal layout, but the current large images all sit on the left.','Redistribute the visual weight. Explain that balance can be symmetrical or asymmetrical.','Describe balance without requiring perfect symmetry.','Do you want a symmetrical layout or a less formal balanced arrangement?','Less formal, but the left side should not carry all the weight.','I can balance the larger image with smaller elements on the other side. That may feel less formal than a perfectly symmetrical layout. Shall I show both?','A portfolio cover has a large photograph in one corner. Suggest how to balance the composition without simply duplicating the photo.','I could balance the photograph with a smaller text group on the opposite side. I would compare the visual weight rather than duplicate the image automatically.'],
['proximity','Proximity','Relationship: closer together','The date and time are closer together in B.','The date and time are together.','Putting related details close together helps viewers understand their relationship before reading every word.','People miss the time because it is far from the date.','I grouped the date and time together.','Please do the same for the location and address.','Which items belong together in this invitation?',['The date and time.','The date and an unrelated decorative dot.','Any random pair of words.'],0,['Yes. Together they tell viewers when to come.','The dot does not explain the date.','Grouping should follow a meaningful relationship.'],['The date and time are closer together.','The date and time is closer together.','The date and time are more closer together.'],0,'The combined subject is plural: “are”. Use “closer” without “more”.','You want date and time grouped together, and location with street address.','Explain the two related groups and keep a gap between them.','Show relationships through proximity.','Which details should readers understand as one group?','Date with time, and location with its street address.','I’ll place the related details close together and leave a larger gap between the two groups. Does that match the way customers will use the information?','A restaurant menu puts each price far from its dish name. Recommend a grouping change and explain its function.','I would keep each dish name close to its price so readers can connect them quickly. Should descriptions stay directly below each name?'],
['repetition','Repetition','Consistency: the same … across …','The same colors and type style repeat across the cards in B.','B repeats the same style.','Repeating selected visual features creates consistency across different items without requiring every item to carry the same content.','These three posts feel like different brands.','I repeated the same colors and type style across the set.','Keep each post’s message different, though.','What repeats in B while the messages differ?',['The visual style.','Exactly the same post content.','The opening date everywhere, regardless of truth.'],0,['Yes. Consistent style can connect different messages.','The content is allowed to change.','Repetition is not permission to reuse inaccurate facts.'],['The same style repeat across the cards.','The same style repeats across the cards.','The same style repeating across the cards.'],1,'Singular “style” takes “repeats”.','You need three different social posts that still look like one brand.','Repeat selected colors, type, or shapes without making all the content identical.','Create consistency across a set.','Which brand features should stay consistent across the posts?','Keep the same colors and headline style, but the offers are different.','I’ll repeat the colors and headline style while changing the offer text for each post. Would you like a reusable template too?','A course provider needs posters for three different classes. Recommend how they can look connected while keeping their details distinct.','I would repeat the headline style and color system across the posters, while keeping each class’s dates and details accurate.'],
['simplicity','Simplicity','Remove extra …; keep essential …','B removes extra decoration and keeps the essential information.','B keeps the important details.','Simplifying a design can remove competing decoration while preserving the information people actually need.','The poster feels busy. What can we remove?','I removed extra decoration and kept the invitation, date, time, and location.','Good. Those details are essential.','What should stay after simplification?',['The essential information.','Only decoration, with no opening details.','Nothing at all.'],0,['Yes. Simplification should preserve the communication job.','That would lose the information viewers need.','An empty poster would not communicate this invitation.'],['I removed the extra decoration.','I removed the extra informations.','I removed the essential details because they are essential.'],0,'“Decoration” works as an uncountable category here; the useful change removes extras rather than essential details.','You want a less busy poster, but the invitation, date, time, and location all have to remain.','Choose one extra decoration to remove and explain what you preserved.','Simplify without losing the message.','Which information is essential for people to attend?','The invitation, date, time, and location all need to stay.','I can remove extra decoration while keeping those four details. Would you like a version with one simple image instead of several?','A crowded workshop slide contains ornamental borders and a long registration instruction. Explain what you would simplify and what must stay.','I would remove the ornamental borders and shorten the instruction without losing the registration steps. Could you confirm which steps are essential?'],
['function','Function','Usefulness: helps people …','Version B helps people know when and where to come.','B tells people when and where to come.','The design’s function is to help the intended audience understand or do something, so we judge the result against that purpose.','The artwork is attractive, but does it invite people to the opening?','Version B gives the date, time, and location. It helps viewers act on the invitation.','That is the information we need.','Why does B serve this brief better?',['It supplies the details viewers need to attend.','It contains no information.','It guarantees everyone will attend.'],0,['Yes. It serves the intended communication purpose.','B includes date, time, and location.','The design can help communicate; it cannot guarantee attendance.'],['The poster helps people find the event.','The poster helps people finding the event.','The poster help people find the event.'],0,'Use “helps” with singular “poster”; “helps people find” is natural here.','Your goal is opening-day attendance. A beautiful abstract image alone is not giving the practical details.','Ask what viewers need to do, then check whether the design gives them the information to do it.','Judge the design by its communication purpose.','What should the viewer be able to do after seeing the poster?','Know when and where to attend the opening.','I’ll make the date, time, and location clear so viewers can act on the invitation. Could you confirm that attendance is the main goal?','A sign in a clinic is meant to direct patients to reception. The design is beautiful but gives no direction. Explain what its function requires.','The sign needs to help patients find reception. I would add a clear direction and test whether someone can follow it quickly.']
]
for i,x in enumerate(items):
 (visual,title,focus,target,a2,b2,c,ds,follow,q,choices,answer,reasons,rep,ra,rwhy,clientrole,designerrole,mission,qmodel,canswer,tmodel,brief,imodel)=x
 cat='intro' if i==0 else 'elements' if i<8 else 'principles'
 D['foundations'].append(dict(id=f'foundation-{visual}',title=title,focus=focus,visual='foundation-'+visual,concept=visual,category=cat,dialogue=[['Client',c],['Designer',ds],['Client',follow]],target=target,levels={'A2':{'model':a2,'hint':'Name one visible detail and what it does.'},'B1':{'model':target,'hint':'Connect the visible change to its communication purpose.'},'B2':{'model':b2,'hint':'Explain the choice and qualify claims about audience response.'}},chunks=[[focus.split(':')[0],'Notice the complete structure in the model.',target],['the audience','The people who need to understand or use the message.','Who is the audience for this design?'],['to communicate','Explain the purpose of arranging the visuals.','We arrange these elements to communicate the invitation.']],meaning={'question':q,'choices':choices,'answer':answer,'reasons':reasons},repair={'question':'Which sentence would you use here?','choices':rep,'answer':ra,'reason':rwhy},roles={'client':clientrole,'designer':designerrole},mission=mission,success=['Point to a real visible choice.','Explain what the choice does for the message.','Check the client’s goal before confirming.'],question_model=qmodel,client_answer=canswer,transfer_model=tmodel,new_brief=brief,independent_model=imodel,scaffolds={'A2':['I can see ___.','The ___ helps people ___.','Do you need ___?'],'B1':['Describe the change you can point to.','Explain how it helps the message.','Ask about the audience or the requirement.'],'B2':['Connect one element or principle to the communication goal.','Explain a tradeoff or a limit of the interpretation.','Ask for the information needed to judge the result.']}))
p.write_text(json.dumps(D,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2));print('Added 16 foundation rounds: introduction + 7 elements + 8 principles')
